1. PRIMARY OBJECTIVE AND NON-NEGOTIABLE INSTRUCTIONS

You are working on an existing frontend prototype for the Blue Star Employee Recruitment System.

Your task is to improve, extend, and refine the current project into a polished, modern, interactive recruitment management platform. Use the existing deployed website, https://veridexbynodenerds.netlify.app/, as a functional reference for the kind of experience and workflow I want to achieve. Do not copy its visual theme. The Blue Star project must retain its own branding, visual identity, and design direction.

The existing project is the source of truth for its current functionality, architecture, and implemented features.

ABSOLUTE RULE: DO NOT REBUILD THE PROJECT FROM SCRATCH

Before making any changes, inspect the entire existing codebase and understand how the application currently works.

Identify:

* The current framework and project structure.
* The main entry point and routing system.
* All existing pages and components.
* The current login and authentication simulation.
* The existing dashboards and workspaces.
* The current navigation and sidebar behavior.
* The current theme implementation, if any.
* Existing local state management.
* Existing forms, tables, search functionality, filters, buttons, and modals.
* Existing candidate, employee, HR, and recruitment workflows.
* Existing mock data and how it is stored.
* Existing responsive design behavior.
* Current dependencies and available libraries.
* Any existing file upload, notification, or chatbot functionality.

Read the relevant files before editing them. Do not assume that a feature is missing simply because it is not immediately visible.

Create an internal understanding of the existing application’s structure and determine which components can be reused, which need to be modified, and which genuinely need to be added.

Preserve every existing working feature unless a requirement in this prompt explicitly asks you to modify or remove it.

Do not:

* Replace the existing application with a new starter template.
* Delete working components simply because you prefer another implementation.
* Rewrite the entire application when targeted changes would work.
* Change existing login flows unnecessarily.
* Remove current functionality while improving the appearance.
* Change existing data structures without understanding their purpose.
* Replace functional controls with decorative elements.
* introduce unnecessary libraries or dependencies.
* Create duplicate pages or duplicate components for the same functionality.
* Remove features because they are inconvenient to integrate.
* Introduce unnecessary sample records to make a dashboard appear full.
* change working routes without providing an appropriate replacement.
* silently introduce breaking changes to existing functionality.

If an existing feature already satisfies a requirement, reuse it and improve it only where necessary.

If a requested feature conflicts with an existing feature, inspect the implementation, preserve the underlying functionality wherever possible, and make the smallest appropriate change.

REQUIRED WORKING APPROACH

Follow this sequence:

1. Inspect the current project.
2. Identify its architecture and working functionality.
3. Map the requested changes to the existing files and components.
4. Determine which features can be reused.
5. Implement the changes incrementally.
6. Verify the existing features still work.
7. Verify the new features work.
8. Fix any errors introduced by the changes.
9. Provide a concise summary of the modifications and any remaining limitations.

Do not stop after producing a plan if you have the tools and access required to edit the project. Carry out the implementation.

If an essential requirement cannot be completed because of a missing dependency, unavailable file, or architectural limitation, explain the specific limitation rather than silently omitting the feature.

